      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published by the BMJ Publishing Group Limited. For permission to use (where not already granted under a licence) please go to http://www.bmj.com/company/products-services/rights-and-licensing/",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To evaluate the efficacy of an individualised acupuncture protocol for patients with fibromyalgia."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Randomised controlled multicentre trial, blinded to participants and to data analysts. Conducted in three primary care centres in southern Spain. A total of 164 participants aged over 17\u2005years and diagnosed with fibromyalgia were enrolled in this trial; 153 participants completed the study. Participants were randomly assigned to either the real intervention (individualised acupuncture, IA) or the sham intervention (sham acupuncture, SA). In both the IA and SA groups, one session per week (lasting 20\u2005min) was provided, in addition to usual pharmacological treatment. The primary outcome was change in pain intensity at 10\u2005weeks."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Intention-to-treat analysis revealed that the decrease in pain intensity at 10\u2005weeks was greater (p=0.001) in the IA group (-41.0%, 95% CI -47.2% to -34.8%) than in the SA group (-27.1%, 95% CI -33.2% to -20.9%). During the follow-up period, significant differences (p<0.01) in favour of the IA group persisted at 12\u2005months (IA: -19.9%, 95% CI -24.6% to -15.1%; vs SA: -6.2%, 95% CI -11.2% to -1.2%)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Individualised acupuncture treatment in primary care in patients with fibromyalgia proved efficacious in terms of pain relief, compared with placebo treatment. The effect persisted at 1\u2005year, and its side effects were mild and infrequent. Therefore, the use of individualised acupuncture in patients with fibromyalgia is recommended."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "TRIAL REGISTRATION NUMBER",
            "content": "ISRCTN60217348."
          }
        ]
      },
